WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.620
Good to have you here. This is unintended consequences, Episode 3, from May 5th, 2026.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.880
Every story we cover starts with someone who had a reasonable plan.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:18.960
In 1921, Thomas Midgley Jr. added tetraethyl lead to gasoline to silence engine knock.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.180
He never imagined the additive would raise blood led levels in entire generations

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.800
and measurably lower cognitive performance worldwide.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:35.160
Midgley was working in a Dayton, Ohio laboratory on December 9th, 1921.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:42.080
He first demonstrated that a few drops of tetraethyl lead in a test engine eliminated the sharp

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.180
metallic ping that destroyed pistons and wasted fuel. Within three years, the same compound was

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:56.400
being added to gasoline sold across the United States. It was marketed under the reassuring brand name

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.780
Ethyl. The engineers who championed the additive believed they had solved a mechanical problem

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:08.720
that stood in the way of faster, more efficient automobiles. Instead, they created a new,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.240
invisible form of pollution that would accumulate in soil, dust, and human bone for most of the 20th

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.920
century. The problem, Midgley and his colleagues at General Motors Research, were trying to solve

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:29.200
was real and urgent. Early internal combustion engines suffered from knock. This was a spontaneous

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:36.260
detonation of the fuel air mixture. It limited compression ratios. It reduced power. It often

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:43.380
cracked cylinder heads. Charles F. Kettering, vice president of research at General Motors and Midgley's

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.880
boss, had already spent years searching for an anti-knock agent. He wanted American cars to

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:58.000
compete with more refined European designs. By 1922, the automobile industry was expanding rapidly.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.220
Any improvement in fuel efficiency or engine durability promised large commercial returns.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:12.920
Midgley tested hundreds of compounds. He tried iodine, aniline, selenium, and ethanol. He discovered

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:20.560
that tetraethyl lead, a little known organic compound first synthesized in 1853, worked dramatically

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:27.800
at concentrations as low as one part per thousand. At the time, lead was already a familiar industrial

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:34.640
material used in paint, pipes, and batteries. No one in the laboratory had reason to suspect that

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.980
dispersing it through exhaust pipes would create a different order of hazard. The decision to pursue

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:48.620
lead was therefore a rational, data-driven choice made by competent chemists. They were measured against

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:55.960
the performance metrics of 1921 engines, rather than the environmental metrics of later decades.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:03.160
General Motors formed a joint venture with standard oil and DuPont to manufacture and distribute

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:10.860
the additive. Production of ethyl fluid began in 1923 at a DuPont plant in deep-watered New Jersey.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:19.120
The first public sales occurred that same year in Dayton and Indianapolis. By 1924, the product was

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:25.620
being blended into gasoline at refineries from Bayway, New Jersey to El Segundo, California.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.520
Early advertising emphasized smoother engine operation and greater mileage.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.720
The word lead was deliberately omitted from pump labels and advertising copy.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.720
A handful of public health experts raised alarms almost immediately.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:52.240
In 1922, the US Public Health Service asked for more data. In 1925, a conference convened by the

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:59.480
Surgeon General heard testimony that workers at the Bayway refinery had died after inhaling tetraethyl

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:06.460
fumes. Yet the industry argued that the concentrations reaching the general public would be negligible.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:13.360
Regulators allowed continued sale provided warning labels were attached to the concentrated fluid

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:20.480
itself. Within a decade, virtually every new car sold in the United States ran on leaded gasoline.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.320
The practice spread to Europe and Latin America through licensing agreements.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.940
The causal chain began with the simple fact that lead burned an engine emerged

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:38.280
thus fine particles of lead oxide and lead chloride. These particles remained suspended in exhaust gases.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:45.420
They settled as dust along road sides and in urban neighborhoods. Tetraethyl lead allowed higher

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:53.580
compression ratios. Engines became more powerful and cars more numerous. Annual US gasoline consumption

00:04:53.580 --> 00:05:01.860
rose from roughly 8 billion gallons in 1925 to more than 50 billion by 1950. This multiplied the

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:08.380
total lead released each year. Children absorbed the metal through hand-to-mouth contact with contaminated

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:15.560
soil. They also absorbed it through inhalation of street dust. Their rapidly growing nervous systems

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:22.960
proved especially sensitive. By the late 1950s, average blood lead levels in American children living

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:30.560
in cities exceeded 20 micrograms per deciliter. That was four times a threshold later judged safe

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.820
by the Centers for Disease Control. Lunditudinal studies begun in the 1970s by Herbert Needleman at

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:44.580
Harvard showed consistent dose-dependent reductions in IQ, attention span, and impulse control.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:52.160
Those findings were later replicated in multiple countries. Some econometric analyses have linked

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:58.820
the rise and subsequent fall of lead exposure to corresponding shifts in crime rates two decades later.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:06.160
The precise magnitude remains debated. A second less noticed consequence was the masking effect.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:12.240
Because lead-improved engine performance, refiners were able to maintain high-octane fuel from

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.320
lower-quality crude. This delayed investment in catalytic cracking and other cleaner refining

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:25.340
technologies. The same chemists who introduced lead-ed gasoline, Thomas Midgley, later developed

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:32.500
dichloro-difloro-methane as a refrigerant. The two inventions together illustrate how a single

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:38.600
laboratory's solutions to immediate technical problems could generate long-lived atmospheric and

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:47.880
neurological harms. Scientific consensus shifted gradually. Claire Patterson's 1965 paper Nature

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:54.620
demonstrated that industrial lead had increased environmental concentrations by orders of magnitude

00:06:54.620 --> 00:07:01.900
above natural background levels. The 1970 Clean Air Act gave the Environmental Protection Agency

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:10.100
authority to regulate fuel additives. In 1973, the agency announced a phased reduction schedule tied

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:15.300
to the introduction of catalytic converters. Those converters were poisoned by lead.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.780
Leaded gasoline was fully banned for on-road use in the United States by 1996.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:27.480
Similar phase-outs occurred in Europe during the 1980s and 1990s.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:33.500
They also occurred in most developing countries after 2000 under United Nations guidance.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:40.900
Blood-led levels in US children fell more than 90 percent between 1976 and 2010.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:48.600
Researchers estimate that the average American child born after 2000 gained between two and

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:55.880
five IQ points relative to earlier cohorts. The phase-out itself created a new, smaller problem.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:02.120
Older vehicles and aircraft that still require leaded fuel continue to emit the metal.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:10.000
Legacy contamination in urban soils remains a localized hazard. No substitute anti-knock additive

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:16.740
has matched tetraethyl lead's combination of effectiveness and low cost. That is why small

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:25.380
amounts are still permitted in some general aviation gasoline. The leaded gasoline story shows

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:31.280
that an additive introduced to solve one narrow engineering constraint can be distributed so widely

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:37.700
that its cumulative biological effects become impossible to ignore only after an entire infrastructure

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:44.380
has been built around it. It also illustrates the limits of acute toxicity testing when the hazard is

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:50.380
chronic, low dose, and dispersed through everyday activity rather than concentrated in factories.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.640
Chloro-fluoro carbons, in midgely's case, offer one example.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:01.920
Their unintended consequences appear on a different time scale and in a different environmental

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:09.220
compartment. When evaluating any new additive, fuel, or material today, the relevant question is

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:14.700
not only whether it works in the engine or the device. The deeper question is whether the total

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:20.900
quantity that will ultimately be released has been tested for effects than may take decades to

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:27.320
measure in children or in the atmosphere. Consider the current push to introduce new synthetic fuels

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:34.600
or battery materials at global scale. Engineers can show clear performance gains in the lab or in

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:40.840
a single vehicle. Yet the harder task is to model what happens when billions of devices eventually

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:47.860
release those substances into air, soil, or water. Regulators today face the same pressure

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:53.440
in midgely's team encountered. They must weigh immediate commercial and mechanical benefits

00:09:53.440 --> 00:10:00.380
against diffuse long-term biological costs. The difference is that we now have tools to run

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:07.600
those planetary-scale tests before widespread adoption. The open issue is whether those tools will

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:13.820
be used early enough to prevent another slow accumulation that only becomes visible in the next

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:23.320
generation's health data. Fast. That wraps today's case. The lessons travel further when you

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:28.120
share them. Send this episode to someone who'd find it useful. We'll see you tomorrow.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:35.420
This podcast is curated by Patrick but generated using AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:41.980
The primary reason to do this is I unfortunately don't have the time to be consistent with generating

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:48.180
all the content and wanted to focus on creating consistent and regular episodes for all the themes

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.860
that I enjoy and I hope others do as well.
